Question Title: Should the salt of a bcrypt hashed password be stored separately | Tags: password-management, .net, bcrypt | Answer: No, as you have seen, the salt is part of the resulting hash value (the 22 characters after the cost factor). The BCrypt.Verify() function will extract the salt from the stored hash, so it is pointless to store it separately. You can call the function with only one parameter, the library will then generate a salt and the cost factor on its own: string passwordHash = BCrypt.HashPassword(password);
// or if you want to specify the cost factor
passwordHash = BCrypt.HashPassword(password, 12);

